    "result_text": "All 36 tests pass across every checkpoint (Cp01\u2013Cp26), including the new `Cp26Tests` (telephone-country-length) and the existing telephone tests. `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe change enforces that an E.164 telephone's **national-number length matches its country code** on the owner-create path.\n\n**`TelephoneE164.java`** \u2014 added `nationalLengthValid(String e164)`. After the country code, it requires `+61` \u2192 9 national digits and `+1` \u2192 10 national digits; any other country code passes (only the existing generic 8\u201315-digit bound applies to those). It checks the longer `61` prefix before `1`, which is safe since a `+61` number never starts with `1` and a `+1` number never starts with `61`.\n\n**`NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java`** \u2014 the create-path step now throws `InvalidTelephoneException` (\u2192 400) when `toE164` returns null **or** `nationalLengthValid` fails.\n\n### Design note\nI deliberately kept the country-length check **out** of the shared `toE164()` and put it only in the create/normalize step. `toE164()` is also reused by `CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique` to normalize *stored* owners for duplicate comparison \u2014 including the NANP seed rows like `6085551023` (10 digits, no `+`), which the default-`+61` logic turns into `+616085551023` (10 national digits). Folding the length rule into `toE164()` would have made those stored values normalize to `null` and silently changed the uniqueness comparison. Scoping the new validation to the incoming request keeps that behavior intact while fully satisfying the spec: reject a create with 400 when the national length is wrong for the country.\n\nThis catches the meaningful new cases (e.g. a `+61` number with 10 national digits, or `+1` with 11 \u2014 both pass the old generic 8\u201315 rule but are now correctly rejected), and `Cp26`'s `+61 123` still returns 400.",
